# 01 - Introduction to Jenkins

## What is Jenkins?

Jenkins is an open-source automation server used to automate software development tasks, including building, testing, and deploying applications.

It is widely used in DevOps to implement Continuous Integration (CI) and Continuous Delivery or Deployment (CD).

## Why Do We Use Jenkins?

Without automation, developers and operations engineers may need to perform repetitive tasks manually.

Jenkins helps automate these tasks through pipelines.

### Example

Imagine a developer updates an application's code and pushes the changes to GitHub.

Jenkins can automatically:

1. Fetch the latest code from GitHub.
2. Build the application.
3. Run automated tests.
4. Build a Docker image.
5. Deploy the application to a server.

This process reduces manual work and helps teams deliver software more consistently.

## What Is CI/CD?

### Continuous Integration (CI)

Continuous Integration is the practice of frequently integrating code changes into a shared repository and automatically building and testing those changes.

### Continuous Delivery (CD)

Continuous Delivery ensures that software changes are automatically built, tested, and prepared for release.

### Continuous Deployment (CD)

Continuous Deployment automatically releases changes to production after they pass the required checks.

## Jenkins in DevOps

Jenkins can integrate with tools such as:

| Tool | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Git | Version control |
| GitHub | Source code hosting |
| Maven | Java application builds |
| Gradle | Application builds |
| Docker | Containerization |
| SonarQube | Code quality analysis |
| Kubernetes | Container orchestration |
| AWS | Cloud infrastructure and deployment |
| Terraform | Infrastructure as Code |

These integrations depend on the tools and plugins configured for your project.

## How Jenkins Works

A basic Jenkins workflow looks like this:

```text
Developer
    |
    v
GitHub Repository
    |
    v
Jenkins Pipeline
    |
    v
Build Application
    |
    v
Run Tests
    |
    v
Build Docker Image
    |
    v
Deploy Application
```

Not every pipeline needs all these stages. The pipeline depends on the application and deployment requirements.

## Important Jenkins Concepts

| Concept | Explanation |
|---|---|
| Jenkins Controller | Coordinates jobs and manages Jenkins configuration |
| Agent | Executes pipeline tasks |
| Job | A configured task or automation workflow |
| Build | One execution of a job or pipeline |
| Pipeline | A sequence of automated stages |
| Jenkinsfile | A file that defines a pipeline as code |
| Plugin | An extension that adds functionality |
| Workspace | Directory used by a job to work with files |
| Credentials | Securely stored authentication information |

## Advantages of Jenkins

- Automates repetitive tasks.
- Supports CI/CD pipelines.
- Integrates with many DevOps tools.
- Supports pipeline definitions as code.
- Provides build logs and execution history.
- Supports distributed builds using agents.

